    englishChapters: [
      [
        'During the mid-fifteenth century, Telangana and coastal Andhra were contested between the declining Recherla Velama chiefs, the expansionist Bahmani Sultanate of Bidar, and the Vijayanagara Empire. When the Bahmani Sultan Humayun Shah besieged Devarakonda in 1459 CE, the Velama rulers appealed to Emperor Kapilendra Deva for military intervention.',
        'Kapilendra Deva entrusted this pivotal Deccan theater to his eldest son, Kumara Hamvira Deva, whose rapid march transformed the balance of power across peninsular India.'
      ],
      [
        'Contemporary inscriptions—including the Warangal Fort Inscription of 1460 CE and the Munnur Tamil Inscription of 1464 CE—identify Kumara Hamvira Deva as the eldest son of Kapilendra Deva and father of Dakshina Kapilesvara Kumara Mahapatra, the viceroy who led Gajapathi forces to the Kaveri river.',
        'His Sanskrit and Telugu royal titles (Hamvira Rautaraya / Ambira) reflect his standing as principal heir-apparent during the zenith of Kapilendra Deva’s military expansion.'
      ],
      [
        'Hamvira’s campaigns traversed the crucial fortress belt linking eastern Odisha to the Deccan plateau: Rajahmundry on the Godavari, Kondavidu in Guntur, Khammammet, Devarakonda, Warangal (Orugallu), and the approaches to Bidar, while his son Dakshina Kapilesvara secured Udayagiri, Chandragiri, Kanchipuram, and Srirangam.',
        'Controlling this hill-and-river citadel corridor gave the Suryavamshi Gajapathis unmatched strategic reach across both coastal plains and plateau trade routes.'
      ],
      [
        'Trained directly in the Paika-Gajapathi military tradition, Kumara Hamvira specialized in rapid relief marches, siege operations against basalt hill forts, and coordinated use of Odra war-elephants and infantry archers against Deccan cavalry.',
        'Before 1460 CE he had already proven his command ability during the consolidation of Kondavidu and the Krishna-Godavari doab.'
      ],
      [
        'In 1459–1460 CE, Hamvira relieved Devarakonda by routing the Bahmani besieging army and captured the historic Kakatiya fortress of Warangal on 2 February 1460 CE, where he installed Raghudeva Narendra Mahapatra as governor and incised a dated victory inscription on the fort gateway.',
        'Following Humayun Shah’s death in 1461 CE, Hamvira launched an audacious strike deep into Bahmani territory toward Bidar, compelling the Deccan Sultanate onto the defensive while Gajapathi arms simultaneously triumphed in the Tamil plains.'
      ],
      [
        'Hamvira operated in close partnership with the Recherla Velama chieftains (Lingama Nayaka) and senior Gajapathi ministers like Raghudeva Narendra Mahapatra. His younger half-brother Purushottama Deva emerged as his dynastic rival when Kapilendra Deva designated Purushottama as successor near the Krishna river in 1466 CE.',
        'Persian chroniclers (Tabataba in Burhan-i-Maasir and Ferishta) also document his later diplomatic dealings with Bahmani Sultan Muhammad Shah III and Minister Mahmud Gawan.'
      ],
      [
        'Bypassed for the Cuttack throne in 1466 CE, Hamvira refused to accept disenfranchisement and fought a protracted succession war (1466–1476 CE). Seeking leverage against Purushottama Deva, he allied with Muhammad Shah III Bahmani around 1471–1472 CE, briefly occupying Cuttack before Purushottama rallied loyal Odra forces and reclaimed the capital around 1472–1476 CE.',
        'During this turmoil, Rajahmundry and Kondavidu temporarily passed to the Bahmanis, though Purushottama later recovered them and reconciled with Hamvira by granting him the strategic estate of Kimidi (Paralakhemundi) or coastal fiefs according to regional traditions.'
      ],
      [
        'Hamvira’s historical reality is anchored in indisputable primary records: the Warangal Fort Inscription dated Saka 1381 (2 February 1460 CE, published in Epigraphia Indica Vol. XXXIII), the Munnur and Tirukkoyilur inscriptions of his son Dakshina Kapilesvara (1464 CE), and independent Persian chronicles (Burhan-i-Maasir and Tarikh-i-Ferishta) calling him "Ambira".',
        'These cross-verified Hindu epigraphs and Indo-Persian histories establish his chronology with high precision.'
      ],
      [
        'Critical scholarship evaluates both sides of the 1466 succession crisis without anachronistic moralizing. Epigraphy confirms Hamvira was the military pillar of Kapilendra’s empire; Madala Panji accounts justify Purushottama’s elevation through divine sanction of Lord Jagannatha, likely reflecting court factionalism over maternal lineage or royal preference.',
        'While his tactical alliance with Bidar cost the empire temporary control of southern Andhra, historians also note his later reconciliation and the continued service of his line.'
      ],
      [
        'Kumara Hamvira Deva remains celebrated in Odisha and Andhra military historiography as the conqueror of Warangal and the chief general who carried the Suryavamshi Gajapathi standard into the heart of Telangana and the Deccan.',
        'His documented lineage connects directly to Martial Heritage (CH-0017), Royal Heritage (CH-0024), and the historical fortification networks preserved across coastal Andhra and southern Odisha.'
      ]
    ],
