    englishChapters: [
      [
        'At the dawn of the nineteenth century, the Khurda kingdom—custodian of the Gajapathi throne and the ritual stewardship of Lord Jagannatha at Puri—stood caught between Maratha Nagpuri tribute demands and the rapidly advancing British East India Company.',
        'In 1803 CE, British commander Colonel George Harcourt sought safe passage through Khurda territory to expel the Marathas from Cuttack, promising to restore four coveted Parganas (Rahang, Serai, Chabiskud, and Lembai) and pay one lakh rupees to Khurda in return.'
      ],
      [
        'Jayakrushna Rajguru Mahapatra was born on 29 October 1739 CE at Biraharekrushnapur, a traditional Brahmin sasana village near Puri, to Chand Rajguru and Haramani Devi. Trained deeply in Sanskrit statecraft, dharma-shastra, and martial administration, he inherited the respected hereditary office of royal preceptor (Rajguru) to the Khurda court.',
        'His reputation for incorruptibility and administrative resolve led Raja Dibyasingha Deva II to entrust the guardianship of his minor son, Mukunda Deva II, to Jayee Rajguru.'
      ],
      [
        'Khurda’s political heart lay at the fortified capital of Khurda Garh beneath Barunei Hill, surrounded by dense bamboo thickets, stone ramparts, and village Paika strongholds across Pipili, Delang, Gangapada, and Tangiapada.',
        'Control of the four disputed Parganas—especially Lembai and Rahang around Puri—was vital both for Khurda’s fiscal survival and for preserving direct royal access to the Puri Jagannatha temple.'
      ],
      [
        'Following the death of Raja Dibyasingha Deva II in 1798 CE, Jayee Rajguru assumed effective regency as Chief Minister and Commander-in-Chief on behalf of the young Raja Mukunda Deva II. He consolidated Paika loyalties, checked internal court intrigues, and negotiated firmly with outside powers to preserve Khurda’s autonomy.',
        'When the British occupied Cuttack in October 1803 and subsequently refused to honor Colonel Harcourt’s written assurances regarding the four Parganas and promised payment, Rajguru personally led a delegation of 2,000 armed Paikas to Cuttack in March 1804 to demand justice.'
      ],
      [
        'Rebuffed by Colonel Harcourt with only a fraction of the promised sum and a demand for total submission, Jayee Rajguru mobilized Khurda’s Paika militia, forged contacts with neighboring chiefs (Kanika, Kujang, and Maratha remnants), and reasserted Khurda’s traditional revenue collection in the four Parganas during autumn 1804.',
        'British authorities responded with a full military invasion of Khurda in November–December 1804, deploying modern artillery against the Paika defenders of Pipili, Delang, and Barunei Fort.'
      ],
      [
        'Jayee Rajguru worked closely with the young Raja Mukunda Deva II, local Dalabeheras and Paika captains, and sympathetic Garhjat feudatories like the Rajas of Kanika and Kujang who resented British annexation.',
        'Even against overwhelming colonial firepower, his strategic coordination held British troops outside Barunei Fort for weeks before treachery and heavy bombardment breached the defenses.'
      ],
      [
        'After the fall of Barunei Fort in December 1804 and the subsequent capture of Raja Mukunda Deva II and Jayee Rajguru in early 1805, both were removed to Midnapore (Medinipur) in Bengal for trial. During the British tribunal proceedings, Jayee Rajguru fearlessly assumed sole responsibility for the uprising, declaring that the young Raja was innocent and had acted only under his counsel.',
        'Convicted of waging war against the British East India Company, Jayee Rajguru was executed on 6 December 1806 CE at Baghitota in Medinipur—an act of colonial brutality remembered vividly across Odisha.'
      ],
      [
        'Jayee Rajguru’s resistance and trial are preserved in primary British archival records: Colonel George Harcourt’s official despatches (1804–1805), Fort William Political and Judicial Consultations (1805–1806), and the Bengal Revenue/Judicial Proceedings preserved in the National Archives of India and Odisha State Archives.',
        'These colonial records corroborate his uncompromising stand on sovereignty, his defense of Raja Mukunda Deva II during trial, and the exact date of his execution (6 December 1806).'
      ],
      [
        'Historians distinguish verified archival documentation of his 1804–1806 struggle and execution from certain later popular retellings regarding the exact physical details of the execution method (historical records confirm hanging/execution at Baghitota, while vivid banyan-branch accounts belong primarily to regional oral memory).',
        'Separating documentary trial records from oral dramatizations strengthens rather than diminishes the historical stature of his sacrifice.'
      ],
      [
        'Shaheed Jayee Rajguru’s martyrdom ignited deep resentment across Khurda and directly inspired Baxi Jagabandhu and the Paikas to rise eleven years later in the 1817 Paika Rebellion. Today he is honored nationwide as one of India’s earliest recorded martyrs against British rule.',
        'Within our portal, his life bridges Royal Khurda Stewardship (CH-0024), Paika Martial Defense (CH-0017), and Classical Administrative Scholarship (CH-0025).'
      ]
    ],
